According to IS 516, cube specimens of 150*150*150 mm were Used to evaluate the concrete’s compressive strength. After casting, the specimens were allowed in the moulds for a full day before being demolded and cured in potable water until they reached testing ages of 7, 14, and 28 days. In order to evaluate both the development of early-age strength and the typical 28-day compressive strength, these curing dates were selected. Specimens were surface-dried and then undergone tested using a calibrated 2000 kN compression testing apparatus. Following continuous application of the load until failure, the compressive strength was calculated as the ultimate load by a loaded cross-sectional area, as indicated by Eq. ( 1 ). To assess the influence of basalt fibres, the identical curing and testing conditions were applied to all fiber-reinforced mixtures. 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
